          %h2#statement Statement
          %p
            The
            %em Cantor–Schroeder–Bernstein
            theorem says that the usual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order+relation"} order relation
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinalities"} cardinalities
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antisymmetric+relation"} antisymmetric
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \leq Y
            if there exists a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon X \to Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \leq Y
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \leq X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            of sets
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \cong Y
          %p
            The result is really only interesting in the absence of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi AC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AC
            ). With
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi AC
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AC
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-ordering+theorem"} well-ordering theorem
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            — indeed, it is actually
            %em equivalent
            to excluded middle (at least assuming the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+infinity"} axiom of infinity
          %h2#proof Proof
          %p
            We prove that the Cantor–Schroeder–Bernstein theorem holds in a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+topos"} Boolean topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistically
            valid, in that it fails in some
            = succeed "es," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %mo •
                      %mo →
                      %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{\bullet \to \bullet}
            (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arrow+category"} arrow category
            of
            = succeed ");" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#counterexample"}
            below.
          %p
            Throughout we use ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set-theoretic
            reasoning which can be translated into the formal theory of toposes. (This can be formalized via the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mitchell%E2%80%93Benabou+language"} Mitchell–Benabou language
          %p
            First, let’s try a little pedagogy. Somehow functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi h
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h: X \to Y, h^{-1}: Y \to X
            are to be cooked up from injections
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: X \to Y
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g: Y \to X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            is to be defined as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            at least part of the time, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi h
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h^{-1}
            as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            another part of the time. An ideal situation would be to have a set-up
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo ∼
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: A \stackrel{\sim}{\longrightarrow} B
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟵
                    %mo ∼
                  %mi D
                  %mo :
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \stackrel{\sim}{\longleftarrow} D: g
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A, C
            are complementary subsets in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B, D
            are complementary subsets in
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            could be defined as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g^{-1}
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            If we have this situation, then apparently
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B = f(A)
            (the direct image of
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo =
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = \neg B = \neg f(A)
            (the complement of
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(A)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo =
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C = g(D) = g(\neg f(A))
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A, C
            are required to be complementary, so we would need
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = \neg g(\neg f(A)).
          %p
            In other words,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            would be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixed+point"} fixed point
            of a suitable operation built from direct image and complementation operators. In fact, if we find such a fixed point
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p Perhaps the simplest fixed-point theorem for this purpose on the market is
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_knastertarski_fixedpoint_theorem Lemma (Knaster–Tarski fixed-point theorem)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi\colon P X \to P X
              be an order-preserving map. Then there exists
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X
              for which
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(A) = A
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              be the (internal)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection"} intersection
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi T
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi P
                      %mi X
                      %mo :
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U = \{T \in P X : \phi(T) \leq T\}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \leq T
              for every
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(A) \leq \phi(T) \leq T
              for every
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(A) \leq A
              by definition of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
              again, we get
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \phi(A) \leq \phi(A)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(A)
              belongs to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \leq \phi(A)
              by definition of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          #term.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The preceding proof is valid in
              %em any
              topos (and so holds for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              even intuitionistically). It can be seen as a specialization to posets of a result of Lambek on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+algebra"} initial
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+an+endofunctor"} algebra of an endofunctor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is (by construction) an initial fixed point.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_of_cantorschroederbernstein Proof of Cantor–Schroeder–Bernstein
            %p
              Suppose given two monos
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: X \to Y
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo :
                      %mi Y
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g: Y \to X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_f: P X \to P Y
              denote direct image or
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantification"} existential quantification
              along
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg_X: P X \to P X
              denote
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negation"} negation
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi g
                    %msub
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi Y
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi = \neg_X \exists_g \neg_Y \exists_f: P X \to P X
            %p
              is order-preserving, and so has a fixed point
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              by the Knaster-Tarski lemma. Now define
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h: X \to Y
              by the rule
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi h
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mtext if
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∈
                            %mi A
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi h
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi g
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mtext if
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∉
                            %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    h(x) &amp; = &amp; f(x) &amp; \text{ if }\; x \in A \\
                    h(x) &amp; = &amp; g^{-1}(x) &amp; \text{ if }\; x \notin A
                    }
            %p
              (the multi-line definition is where we use the Boolean condition). The second line makes sense because
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg A
              is in the image of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
              is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi j
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi f
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mtext if
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi y
                            %mo ∈
                            %msub
                              %mo ∃
                              %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi A
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi j
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %mi g
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mtext if
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi y
                            %mo ∉
                            %msub
                              %mo ∃
                              %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi A
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    j(y) &amp; = &amp; f^{-1}(y) &amp; \; \text{if} \; y \in \exists_f(A) \\
                    j(y) &amp; = &amp; g(y) &amp; \; \text{if} \; y \notin \exists_f(A)
                    }
            %p
              That
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
              is inverse to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
              uses the fact that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi A
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mo ∃
                        %mi g
                      %mo ¬
                      %msub
                        %mo ∃
                        %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg A = \exists_g \neg \exists_f(A)
          %p
            This classic proof is substantially the proof given in Johnstone’s
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elephant"} Elephant
            %em strictly speaking
            necessary, i.e., the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+excluded+middle"} principle of excluded middle
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi EM
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} EM
            ) does not logically follow from the Cantor–Schroeder–Bernstein statement since, for example, using notation as above, monic endomorphisms like
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \circ g: Y \to Y
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \circ f: X \to X
            are isomorphisms in
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi FinSet
                    %mi C
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinSet^C,
          %p
            but this is a non-Boolean, non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck
            topos provided that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+category"} finite category
            that is not a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo =
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C = \mathbf{2}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi EM
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} EM
            is certainly the most natural supposition to make. In fact EM does constructively follow from the Cantor-Schroeder-Bernstein statement provided that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
            exists (for instance in a Grothendieck topos); see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#PB19"} Pradic and Brown, 2019
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Freyd94"} Freyd 1994
          %h3#alt Alternative construction of a fixed point
          %p
            In some schools of thought, the proof using the Knaster-Tarski lemma would be criticized because that lemma makes use of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/impredicative"} impredicative
            construction. However, the application made of it in the proof of the CSB theorem is only to ensure that the operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi g
                  %msub
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi Y
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg_X \exists_g \neg_Y \exists_f: P X \to P X
            has a fixed point. This objection can be countered by shopping around for a different fixed-point theorem, one which is predicatively and constructively valid.
          %p
            A time-honored way of constructing a fixed point of an operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            is by taking a limit of a sequence of iterates of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            that converges, provided that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            preserves the limit. To this end, we find that specializing Adámek’s theorem (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+algebra+of+an+endofunctor"} initial algebra of an endofunctor
          #chain.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g: Y \to X
              is monic, then the operator
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_g: P Y \to P X
              preserves limits of inverse chains
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ω
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega^{op} \to P Y
              (i.e. intersections of decreasing sequences).
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              More generally,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_g
              preserves
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+limits"} connected limits
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: P X \downarrow \exists_g(1) \hookrightarrow P X
              to an isomorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ∼
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P Y \stackrel{\sim}{\to} P X \downarrow \exists_g(1)
              (here
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              denotes the top element of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P Y
              = succeed ")," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              preserves connected limits.
            %p
              In more detail: by
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+reciprocity"} Frobenius reciprocity
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi g
                    %mi T
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi S
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo ∧
                    %msup
                      %mi g
                      %mo *
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_g T \wedge S = \exists_g(T \wedge g^\ast S)
              for elements
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P Y
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T = 1
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∃
                        %mi g
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi S
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mo ∃
                        %mi g
                      %msup
                        %mi g
                        %mo *
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_g 1 \wedge S = \exists_g g^\ast S
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi g
                    %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi g
                          %mo *
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∃
                          %mi g
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi g
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X \downarrow \exists_g 1 \stackrel{g^\ast}{\to} P Y \stackrel{\exists_g}{\to} P X \downarrow \exists_g 1
            %p
              is the identity. But since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
              is monic,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi g
                      %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g^\ast \exists_g: P Y \to P Y
              is also the identity, which completes the proof.
          .num_cor
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi g
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi g
                    %msub
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi Y
                    %mo :
                    %mi P
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_g = \neg_X \exists_g \neg_Y: P Y \to P X
              preserves colimits of
              = succeed "-chains." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
          %p
            Naturally the left adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_f
            also preserves such colimits. So by the corollary, the composite
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi g
                  %msub
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi Y
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg_X \exists_g \neg_Y \exists_f: P X \to P X
            preserves colimits of
            = succeed "-chains." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
          %p
            Putting now
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 0
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_0 = 0
            (the bottom element of
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi g
                    %msub
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi Y
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_1 = \neg_X \exists_g \neg_Y \exists_f(0)
          #eq:An.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi g
                  %msub
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi Y
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi f
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_n = (\neg_X \exists_g \neg_Y \exists_f)^n(0),
          %p
            we have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi n
                  %mo ⊆
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_n \subseteq A_{n+1}
            (apply the monotone operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi g
                  %msub
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi Y
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi f
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\neg_X \exists_g \neg_Y \exists_f)^n
            to the inclusion
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 0
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ⊆
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_0 = 0 \subseteq A_1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi g
                  %msub
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi Y
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg_X \exists_g \neg_Y \exists_f
            preserves the union of the chain
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ⊆
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi …
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_0 \subseteq A_1 \subseteq \ldots
          #eq:A.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi g
                  %msub
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi Y
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi f
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = \bigcup_{n \geq 0} (\neg_X \exists_g \neg_Y \exists_f)^n(0),
          %p
            which implies that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a fixed point of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi g
                    %msub
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi Y
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg_X \exists_g \neg_Y \exists_f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is the minimal fixed point, just as in the conclusion of the Knaster-Tarski lemma. (Cf.
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+algebra+of+an+endofunctor"} initial algebra of an endofunctor
          %h3#beta_reduced_proof Beta reduced proof
          %p
            The preceding proofs are sometimes considered too abstract to easily visualize, but this is slightly misleading: the second proof, involving the construction of a minimal fixed point as a countable limit, can be “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/beta-reduced"}> beta-reduced
            ” to produce one of the standard “concrete” proofs.
          %p
            In a nutshell, the minimal fixed point of the operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi g
                  %mo ¬
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg \exists_g \neg \exists_f: P X \to P X
            can be expressed as an alternating series of iterated direct images:
          #eq:series.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (3)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo −
                  %mi g
                  %mi Y
                  %mo +
                  %mi g
                  %mi f
                  %mi X
                  %mo −
                  %mi g
                  %mi f
                  %mi g
                  %mi Y
                  %mo +
                  %mi …
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X - g Y + g f X - g f g Y + \ldots
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -
            stands for set-theoretic difference
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∖
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \setminus
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +
            stands for the union
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cup
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo −
                  %mi g
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X - g Y,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo −
                  %mi g
                  %mi Y
                  %mo +
                  %mi g
                  %mi f
                  %mi X
                  %mo −
                  %mi g
                  %mi f
                  %mi g
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X - g Y + g f X - g f g Y,
          %p
            etc., and the infinite series is interpreted as the countable union of this increasing sequence. Note that we have to be careful about the order of the appearance of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊕
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \oplus
            be the addition in the Boolean
            %em ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X
            (symmetric difference), we could write also the series as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi g
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi g
                  %mi f
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi …
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \oplus g Y \oplus g f X \oplus \ldots
            in the ring, where we do not need to be fussy about order.
          %p Most of this is a routine calculation, which for the most part boils down to the following observation:
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_2 Lemma
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B, D
              are elements of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P Y
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \leq B
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∃
                        %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mo −
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mo ∃
                        %mi g
                      %mi B
                      %mo −
                      %msub
                        %mo ∃
                        %mi g
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_g(B - D) = \exists_g B - \exists_g D
              = succeed ".)" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∃
                        %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_f
          %p
            The proof is left to the reader, but in brief, the injectivity of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            implies that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_g
            preserves binary intersections and relative complements.
          %p From here, if we write
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi g
                  %mo ¬
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi X
                  %mo −
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo −
                  %mi f
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi X
                  %mo −
                  %mi g
                  %mi Y
                  %mo +
                  %mi g
                  %mi f
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi g
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi g
                  %mi f
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg \exists_g \neg \exists_f(B) = X - g(Y - f B) = X - g Y + g f B = X \oplus g Y \oplus g f B,
          %p
            then it is easily verified by induction that, referring to equation
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:An"} (1)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ¬
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi g
                  %mo ¬
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi f
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi n
                  %mn 0
                  %mo =
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mi f
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi j
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊕
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %mi n
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mi g
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi j
                  %mi Y
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_n = (\neg \exists_g \neg \exists_f)^n 0 = \bigoplus_{j=0}^n (g f)^j X \oplus \bigoplus_{j=0}^n g(f g)^j Y.
          %p
            Thus, according to equation
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:A"} (2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is the union of the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_n
            which is how we are interpreting the series
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:series"} (3)
          %p
            Now we set up a comparison with one of the standard proofs involving a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/back-and-forth+argument"} back-and-forth argument
            %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schr%C3%B6der%E2%80%93Bernstein_theorem#Proof"} Wikipedia
            that is attributed to Julius König. The minimal fixed point is a union of finitary approximations
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 1
