                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcap_{n \geq 1} (g f)^n(X)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            that belong to a doubly infinite sequence or a cyclic sequence (in the terminology of the Wikipedia article). As remarked in that article, for such
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            we have an option to define
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h(x)
            as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)
            or
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi g
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g^{-1}(x)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h(x) = f(x)
            for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            belonging to whichever fixed point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is used, which includes points in doubly infinite sequences or cyclic sequences if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is the maximal fixed point. In that case the remaining
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            (belonging to the complement of the maximal fixed point) are mapped to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi g
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g^{-1}(x)
          %h2#failure_in_toposes_and_constructive_mathematics Failure in toposes and constructive mathematics
          #Top.num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              Counterexample
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#counterexample2"}
              below shows that the CSB theorem fails in Brouwer's
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+mathematics"} intuitionistic mathematics
              even for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              (since every function between the sets
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0, 1]
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              must be continuous by Brouwer's continuity principle!). See also the discussion in
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#MM"} Mac Lane-Moerdijk
          #counterexample.num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              As mentioned above, the Cantor-Schroeder-Bernstein theorem fails in the arrow category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Set
                        %mo →
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^\to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0 \to X_1
              between sets and whose morphisms are commutative squares. For example, let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be the object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}
              that takes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} int
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{int}(n/2)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} int
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{int}(x)
              is the greatest integer less than or equal to
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              be the object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g: \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}
              that takes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Int
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Int}((n+1)/2)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Int
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Int}(x)
              is the least integer greater than or equal to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              are non-isomorphic, because
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi g
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g^{-1}(0)
              has cardinality
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              whereas all fibers of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              have cardinality
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: X \to Y
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                    %mo :
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j: Y \to X
              (e.g., define
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_0(n) = n+1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_1(n) = n+1
              for all
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi j
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j_0(n) = n+1
              for
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \gt 0
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi j
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j_0(0) = 0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi j
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j_1(n) = n
              for all
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            Nor can one have internal existence of an isomorphism between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            in this last example, since internal existence implies external existence as soon as the terminal object is (externally) projective.
          #effectivetopos.num_example
            %h6#example_3 Example
            %p
              There is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+topos"} effective topos
              with an injection from the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Baire+space+of+sequences"} Baire space of sequences
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}^\mathbb{N}
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℕ
                        %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}^\mathbb{N}
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere%27s+fixed+point+theorem"} Lawvere's fixed point theorem
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℕ
                        %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}^\mathbb{N}
          %p
            In fact, the CSB theorem is equivalent in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            (with the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+infinity"} axiom of infinity
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/law+of+excluded+middle"} law of excluded middle
            %a{:href => "#PB19"} Pradic and Brown, 2019
            using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+omniscience"} principle of omniscience
            for the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+natural+numbers"} extended natural numbers
          %h2#in_other_categories In other categories
          %p
            The CSB property holds in some other
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            of interest (but arguably fails in many more). Some examples follow:
          #archiemdean.num_example
            %h6#example_4 Example
            %p
              The CSB property holds in the category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Archimedean+ordered+fields"} Archimedean ordered fields
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+homomorphisms"} ring homomorphisms
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
              of the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/powerset"} powerset
              of the real numbers; i.e. the category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Archimedean+ordered+fields"} Archimedean ordered fields
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/powerset"} powerset
              of the real numbers. This remains true in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
              but one has to use the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+real+numbers"} Dedekind real numbers
              as only the Dedekind real numbers are the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
              in the category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Archimedean+ordered+fields"} Archimedean ordered fields
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+homomorphisms"} ring homomorphisms
          #preorder.num_example
            %h6#example_5 Example
            %p
              More generally, the CSB property holds in any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+order"} partial order
              (i.e.
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thin+category"} thin
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gaunt+category"} gaunt category
          #model.num_example
            %h6#example_6 Example
            %p
              The CSB property holds in the category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
              and in the category of
              = succeed "s." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraically+closed+field"} algebraically closed field
              %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/1058/when-does-cantor-bernstein-hold"} MO post
              by John Goodrick, where model-theoretic criteria come into play, sometimes under strengthenings of the notion of monomorphism (e.g.,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+embedding"} elementary embedding
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+monomorphism"} split monomorphism
              %a{:href => "http://settheory.mathtalks.org/wp-content/uploads/2012/06/jonh_goodrick.pdf"} here
              go into more detail, giving connections between CSB and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stability+theory"} stable theories
              in the sense of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shelah"} Shelah
          #counterexample2.num_example
            %h6#counterexample Counterexample
            %p
              On the other hand, the CSB property obviously fails in
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/embeddings"} embeddings
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo ≅
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \cong (0,1) \to [0,1] \to \mathbb{R}
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ≇
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1] \ncong \mathbb{R}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} Grp
              (e.g., the free group on countably many generators embeds in the free group on two generators).
          %p
            More examples and discussion can be found at this Secret Blogging Seminar
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://sbseminar.wordpress.com/2007/10/30/theme-and-variations-schroeder-bernstein/"} post
          %p
            In a
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Gowers"} celebrated work
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Timothy+Gowers"} Timothy Gowers
            gave a negative solution in the case of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+spaces"} Banach spaces
          %p
            Martin Escardó has announced a proof of CSB in the base theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+dependent+type+theory"} Martin-Löf dependent type theory
            with the added assumption of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+extensionality"} function extensionality
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Agda"} Agda
            proof (
            %a{:href => "#Escardo20"}> Escardó 2020
            ). There is a slight subtlety in that “injection” is replaced by “embedding”, where the latter is defined to be a map whose fibres are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsingletons"} subsingletons
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            = succeed "-groupoids." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %h2#name Name
          %p
            The CSB theorem was first stated by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Cantor"} Georg Cantor
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-ordering+theorem"} well-ordering theorem
            %span.newWikiWord
              Felix Bernstein
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Felix+Bernstein"} ?
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                Ernst Schröder
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Ernst+Schr%C3%B6der"} ?
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Dedekind"} Richard Dedekind
            wrote out a proof on 11th July 1887, and only later published in his Nachlass in 1932 (
            %a{:href => "#Dedekind"}> Dedekind 1932
            ), while working on a draft of
            = succeed "," do
              %em Was sind und was sollen die Zahlen?
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li#Dedekind
              %p
